text='''<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>When nearest neighbours help</title><style>body{max-width:1150px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 25px;font:17px/1.55 system-ui;color:#203047}h1,h2{line-height:1.2}h2{margin-top:42px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px}th,td{text-align:right;padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd}th:first-child,td:first-child{text-align:left}img{width:100%;margin:15px 0}.lead{font-size:22px}.note{background:#f0f5f7;padding:18px;border-left:4px solid #538a99}details{margin:15px 0}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:13px}a{color:#246c9b}</style></head><body>
<h1>When nearest-neighbour explanations help—and when they hurt</h1>
<p class="lead">The clearest pattern is reference-label influence: NN context moves judges toward the neighbour’s class. This usually helps when the neighbour is a good class analogue, and can hurt when similarity hides a different label. Final pipeline gains additionally depend on the judge’s starting discrimination, the probe’s headroom, and validation threshold selection.</p>
<p>This is an explanatory analysis of the frozen experiment, not a new inference run. It identifies broad correlations and checks them against existing component/random-reference ablations.</p>
<h2>1. The most consistent association: class alignment</h2>
<p>For every one of the ten judges, adding NN context improves average fixed-50 judge accuracy in the subset whose neighbour shares the target label, and reduces it in the opposite-label subset. This is a post-hoc diagnostic: target gold labels are not available when deploying a system.</p>
'''+img('mechanisms_overview')+'''
<p>Across all ten judges, subgroup macro accuracy changes are +11.83 points for matching labels and −9.88 for opposite labels. The large shifts are partly driven by weak baseline judges. Excluding GLM, Mistral and OLMo as a clearly labelled sensitivity gives +3.97 and −6.99 points. The sign pattern holds separately for all ten judges, so it is not created by that exclusion.</p>
<h2>2. Cosine similarity helps identify analogues, but does not guarantee them</h2>
<p>Neighbour–target label agreement rises from 78.3% in the lowest cosine quintile to 88.6% in the highest, using ranks within each dataset/backbone. Yet the judges’ average movement toward the neighbour label is roughly flat across these quintiles. In the seven-judge sensitivity, opposite-label neighbours become more harmful as similarity rises: fixed-50 accuracy changes from −5.52 to −9.59 points. Matching-label benefits remain around +4 points.</p>
'''+img('similarity_and_length')+'''
<p>The conditional model controls for dataset, judge, backbone, target class, baseline error, probe score, text lengths, lexical overlap and neighbour label/prediction agreement. It retains the alignment-by-similarity interaction. This suggests that high similarity can make a misleading class analogy more persuasive. Raw cosine values from different backbones are not directly comparable.</p>
<h2>3. Dataset size, imbalance and task difficulty</h2>
'''+table(train)+'''
<p><b>Size:</b> across the ten datasets, training size has little monotonic association with tuned F1 gain (Spearman −0.19; leave-one-dataset-out range −0.33 to +0.12). ToxicChat has the smallest training pool and the largest mean gain. WildJailbreak has by far the largest pool but only a small gain. These data do not support a simple “more training data makes NN better” explanation.</p>
<p><b>Imbalance:</b> the positive fraction likewise has no clear monotonic association with gain (Spearman +0.20). However, it strongly affects what label-matching rates mean. FAVA is 97.8% positive in training: neighbours match 97.6% of positive targets but only 9.5% of negative targets. ToxicChat shows the opposite asymmetry: 96.7% negative-class agreement and 58.7% positive-class agreement. A high overall retrieval accuracy can hide poor minority-class analogues.</p>
<p><b>Ceilings:</b> HaluEval and FAVA already have roughly 99.3% and 98.6% baseline pipeline F1 on the common cohort. Improved raw judge scores therefore have very little room to improve the final pipeline. HaluEval’s strong neighbour agreement is not evidence that it must show a large incremental gain.</p>
<p><b>Topic:</b> ToxicChat and BeaverTails show the largest average tuned gains; RAGTruth, WildGuard and ToxiGen have negative averages. But HighStakes ranges from +6.61 points for Qwen to −6.49 for GPT-4o. A coarse safety/factuality/jailbreak label is less informative than the particular task–judge pairing and the task’s boundary cases.</p>
'''+img('dataset_judge_gains')+'''
<details><summary>Class-specific retrieval agreement</summary>'''+table(balance)+'''</details>
<h2>4. Length and the amount of usable evaluation data</h2>
<p>Among the seven stronger-discrimination judges, fixed-50 gains decline from +2.49 accuracy points in the shortest target quartile to +0.95 in the longest. However, the length association weakens after controlling for class alignment, baseline errors and task/model differences (adjusted log-target-length Brier coefficient −0.0027, 95% interval −0.0065 to +0.0012). Neighbour length and lexical overlap also add little independent explanatory signal in this model. Length is a useful descriptive flag, not the leading explanation.</p>
<p>The local prompt preparation checks complete tokenized input length and rejects over-context prompts rather than truncating them. Thus silent local judge-input truncation is not the explanation supported by this pipeline. API serving internals are not fully documented.</p>
<p>Although collection used 500 validation and 500 test cases per dataset/backbone, requiring valid answers from every API/model/condition leaves unequal support. FAVA has only about 89 common test cases per backbone (effective weighted n≈79), whereas ToxicChat has about 449 (effective n≈400). This affects stability and limits interpretation of small differences. Complete local-cohort sensitivity retains the same broad raw-discrimination pattern.</p>
<details><summary>Matched sample counts and effective sample sizes</summary>'''+table(support)+'''</details>
<h2>5. Why judges differ so much</h2>
<p>There are three separable patterns:</p><ol>
<li><b>Different baseline discrimination.</b> Gemma, the Claude aliases and GPT-Astra already distinguish the classes well. GLM, Mistral and OLMo are much weaker under these exact direct-probability prompts. On HaluEval, their baseline scores are systematically higher for correct answers than hallucinated ones. Two saved cases and sixteen raw outputs were audited against exact prompt IDs and checksums: the text instructs positive=hallucination, and the saved generated numbers match the analysis. NN examples sometimes correct this direction, although not universally.</li>
<li><b>Different susceptibility to reference information.</b> GPT-4o loses about 20.1 judge-accuracy points in the opposite-label subset, compared with about 1.6 for Opus. GPT-4o’s average AUROC decreases by about 3.0 points with NN, while several other judges improve. This is stronger evidence of differing behaviour than model size alone.</li>
<li><b>Different value beyond the probe.</b> The validation-selected baseline judge threshold is zero in all 50 GLM and all 50 Mistral cells: every probe flag is accepted. NN improves their raw AUROC considerably, but their scores usually still do not improve the pipeline enough to be used. These tuned results should not be interpreted as evidence that all judges classify equally well.</li></ol>
'''+table(m)+'''
<p>AUROC summarizes ranking rather than a particular cutoff. “Bypass” is the fraction of cells with a judge cutoff of zero. F1 effects are percentage points. The score/policy columns are the symmetric two-factor decomposition obtained by crossing baseline/NN scores with baseline/NN thresholds; they sum exactly to the observed gain. They describe the arithmetic source of the difference, not a causal decomposition of model internals.</p>
<h2>6. Threshold choice can amplify or hide the prompt effect</h2>
<p>Across all 500 common-cohort cells, tuned NN F1 is only +0.14 points higher on average (paired target-cluster 95% interval −0.07 to +0.37). This pooled number conceals both meaningful positive and negative judge/task effects. It is much smaller than improvements in raw fixed-50 decisions.</p>
<p>The previous ToxicChat component analysis is a concrete example: full-NN and text+prediction scores were identical on 465 of 500 test cases. Much of their apparent own-policy difference came from selecting different probe/judge thresholds. At the text+prediction policy, the full-NN scores actually achieved higher F1. Component rankings based solely on separately tuned F1 can therefore be misleading.</p>
<p>The existing local validation-bootstrap analysis also found that the original winning configuration was retained in only a median 73.5% of resamples across 300 cells. That is evidence of policy-selection variability, not a reason to discard any result.</p>
<h2>7. What the component and random-neighbour ablations add</h2>
'''+img('component_ablations')+'''
<p>Across the five previously selected high-benefit pairs, full NN yields +2.44 raw judge-accuracy points, text+label +2.31, label+prediction +2.14, label only +1.85, prediction only +1.81, text only +1.31, and random full examples +1.04. These are fixed-50 judge comparisons on the complete local test samples, not the jointly tuned pipeline results.</p>
<p>Labels alone capture a substantial part of the effect, but are more harmful when mismatched: −10.11 accuracy points versus −6.77 for full NN. That is consistent with text helping the judge assess whether the class cue applies. Random examples can help too, but less than retrieved examples here; their benefit is larger with aligned classes (+2.39 versus −0.66 points). The evidence favours a combination of class information and example relevance, rather than either “text alone explains everything” or “the judge merely copies the label.” These five pairs were chosen for prior NN gains, so their average is not an unbiased estimate for the whole grid.</p>
<h2>8. Backbone differences</h2>
<p>The backbone affects both the probe’s scores/referral decisions and which training example is retrieved. Gemma has the largest mean tuned NN increment in this cohort (+0.30 F1 points); other backbone means are +0.07 to +0.15. The intervals overlap substantially and there is no monotonic size advantage.</p>
<p>Matching all five backbones leaves only 203 shared targets, a highly selected subset with unusual class/label alignment, so its raw averages are unsuitable for a population ranking. A broader within-target/within-judge analysis uses 3,990 targets observed under at least two backbones. A change to a class-aligned neighbour is strongly associated with improved Brier score (coefficient +0.196, interval +0.185 to +0.207), while raw cosine differences add little after adjustment. This supports retrieval choice as a useful observable explanation of backbone variation; it does not establish a superior architecture.</p>
<h2>9. Concrete examples</h2>
<p>The saved examples below were selected deterministically for large cross-judge changes, so they illustrate patterns rather than estimate their prevalence. Some text is offensive or sensitive because these are safety benchmarks. Gold labels are the dataset annotations, not a new adjudication.</p>
'''
examples=json.load(open(O/'matched_examples.json'))
for r in examples:
 if (r['dataset'],r['category']) not in [('beavertails','hurt'),('high_stakes','help'),('high_stakes','hurt'),('toxigen','hurt'),('ragtruth','hurt')]:continue
 text+=f'<details><summary>{esc(r["dataset"])} — {r["category"]}; gold {r["true_label"]}, neighbour {r["nearest_label"]}, cosine {r["nearest_cosine"]:.3f}</summary><p>Target:</p><pre>{esc(json.dumps(r["target_messages"],ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))}</pre><p>Neighbour:</p><pre>{esc(json.dumps(r["neighbor_messages"],ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))}</pre>{table(pd.DataFrame(r["judge_scores"]))}</details>'
text+='''<p>For example, two very similar food-fight responses have opposite BeaverTails labels; four judges switch from correct to incorrect after seeing the positive neighbour. In HighStakes, a routine security review can correctly pull a judge away from treating any mention of health/security as high stakes, while a similar-looking generic cybersecurity reminder can incorrectly downplay a target involving sensitive banking credentials. This is the recurring opportunity and failure mode: a local example can clarify the dataset boundary or import the wrong one.</p>
<h2>Practical conclusion</h2>
<p><b>NN is most promising where the probe leaves useful headroom, the judge can use examples without excessive label following, and retrieved cases reflect the target’s relevant class boundary.</b> It is less useful with an already near-perfect probe, weak or reversed judge scores that add little beyond the probe, or close-looking examples whose labels differ on a subtle detail. Training size and length alone are not good explanations of the observed gains.</p>
<p>The most useful next ablation would target misleading similarity: distinguish topical/surface matches from matches on the decision-relevant feature, or compare agreeing versus conflicting multiple neighbours. A gold-label alignment rule itself cannot be deployed. Any usable selection rule would need to rely on observable neighbour properties and be selected using validation data.</p>
<h2>Scope and reproducibility</h2>
'''
text+=f'<p>50,000 frozen backbone–case records; {verify["common_test"]:,} common valid test backbone–cases representing {verify["common_unique_test_texts"]:,} unique target texts; 178,020 judge–case observations in the primary test analysis. Ten judges × five backbones × ten datasets. Source exports are checksummed. All previous matched precision/recall/F1 values were reproduced to numerical tolerance (maximum difference {verify["max_previous_metric_error"]:.2g}). Training neighbours and source-group separation were verified during export; no exact target–neighbour text duplicates were found.</p>'
text+='''<p>Weights are the frozen inverse inclusion probabilities within each cell; cells receive equal weight. API common-cohort results describe the matched valid subset, not all deployment traffic. Confidence intervals use 2,000 paired Poisson target-cluster bootstrap replicates, keeping cases matched across models/backbones and holding validation-selected thresholds fixed. They do not include threshold-selection uncertainty or uncertainty over an unseen population of datasets. Regression intervals cluster by target text. Dataset-size correlations have only ten task units and are exploratory; none establishes absence of a possible size effect.</p>
<p>Primary results retain all ten judges. Post-hoc seven-judge sensitivities are explicitly labelled and omit the three weakest baseline-discrimination models; complete-local sensitivity retains all six local judges and all collected cases. API names are supplied aliases; exact provider revisions/settings were not supplied. The two-neighbour run was still collecting during this analysis and is not needed for these conclusions. No model calls, probe retraining, changes to frozen splits, or overwriting of judgments were performed.</p>
<p>Machine-readable outputs: <a href="cell_mechanisms.csv">cell effects and decomposition</a> · <a href="paired_cluster_bootstrap.csv">paired uncertainty</a> · <a href="factor_cluster_intervals.csv">factor associations</a> · <a href="conditional_associations.csv">adjusted associations</a> · <a href="dataset_explanatory_table.csv">dataset table</a> · <a href="ablation_summary.csv">ablations</a> · <a href="matched_examples.json">exact examples</a> · <a href="../ANALYSIS_PLAN.json">analysis plan</a>.</p></body></html>'''
(O/'report.html').write_text(text)
print('Created report and four figures')
